WEBVTT


00:00:00.506 --> 00:00:07.506
Hello, welcome back to our course, and let us get started with learning R.

00:00:07.470 --> 00:00:14.470
What is R? R is a programming environment mainly used for data manipulation,

00:00:20.261 --> 00:00:22.261
calculation and graphical display. R can be regarded as an implementation of the S language,

00:00:24.997 --> 00:00:28.997
and it was written by Ihaka and Gentlemen in New Zealand in the 90s.

00:00:29.359 --> 00:00:36.359
R is great, because it's an open source software, and it offers a great variety of

00:00:41.390 --> 00:00:46.390
packages covering all fields of statistics and machine learning. It is also available for Windows, Linux, or Mac.

00:00:47.248 --> 00:00:52.248
We have already learned how to download and install R on

00:00:51.744 --> 00:01:00.744
our machines, and we definitely do not recommend using the R Graphical User Interface

00:01:01.060 --> 00:01:06.060
for the development. Instead, it would be a good idea to use a good

00:01:09.765 --> 00:01:14.765
text editor, or an integrated development environment. In this course, we are going to use RStudio, but

00:01:23.218 --> 00:01:26.218
feel free to use any other of your choice. And we have already seen that particular window, so you might remember that in R studio, we have

00:01:25.669 --> 00:01:32.669
4 panes. We have a pane with an editor, we have a pane with a console,

00:01:32.681 --> 00:01:40.681
we have a pane with these tabs for environment, workspace and the history.

00:01:40.926 --> 00:01:46.926
And we have a pane with tabs for files, plots, packages, help, etc.

00:01:46.876 --> 00:01:54.876
How can we use R? Well, definitely we could use it as a calculator, so we could use it for adding numbers,

00:01:54.715 --> 00:02:01.715
we could use it for subtracting numbers, multiplying numbers, dividing, taking a power of a number,

00:02:08.234 --> 00:02:12.234
doing integer division or modular operators. There are also many pre-programmed functions we could apply to

00:02:19.645 --> 00:02:23.645
numbers, for example SQRT. Apply to a number gives us the square root of that particular number. So here we have a square root of 2.

00:02:24.378 --> 00:02:33.378
Then, also, sine, cosine, and tangent are pre-programmed. So, for example, if we apply SIN

00:02:37.977 --> 00:02:39.977
to pi, we'll get a sine of

00:02:41.444 --> 00:02:45.444
this constant pi, which is also pre-programmed in R.

00:02:51.083 --> 00:02:55.083
We have also R cosine, R sine, etc., exponent, and here, another example of a pre-programmed function is,

00:02:55.406 --> 00:02:59.406
natural logarithm, which is just log applied to a particular number.

00:03:05.253 --> 00:03:10.253
Right. There are also many other functions, for example a floor function applied to pi gives us

00:03:10.163 --> 00:03:15.163
the largest integer, smaller equal to pi. So in this case, it would be 3.

00:03:21.763 --> 00:03:24.763
A ceiling function applied to pi gives us the smallest integer, larger equal than pi. In this case, it would be 4.

00:03:25.222 --> 00:03:31.222
We have also trunc function, which, after applying it to

00:03:40.103 --> 00:03:44.103
pi, would give us an integer, but closer to zero. So basically, here, we can see that 3 is closer to 0 than 4, so definitely we'd get 3.

00:03:51.984 --> 00:03:54.984
But now, attention when it comes to using trunc and floor to negative numbers, because here, once again, trunc, what trunc does, is to

00:03:55.045 --> 00:04:02.045
give us an integer part closer to zero. So if we apply trunc to minus pi,

00:04:10.512 --> 00:04:13.512
integer part closer to 0 is minus 3. Whereas, for floor function, applied to minus pi,

00:04:13.854 --> 00:04:18.854
we have the largest integer smaller equal than minus pi. In this case it's

00:04:23.042 --> 00:04:26.042
minus 4. So, as you can see, trunc and floor applied to negative numbers,

00:04:30.062 --> 00:04:34.062
give us, different outputs, minus 3 minus 4. Whereas trunc and floor applied to positive

00:04:37.003 --> 00:04:40.003
numbers give us the same output. There is also a round function,

00:04:46.360 --> 00:04:51.360
which is basically, applied once again to number pi, and it gives us the nearest integer, in this case, it's 3.

00:04:59.442 --> 00:05:01.442
Right. And now, let us talk about logical operators, so definitely we have a smaller, smaller or equal, larger, larger or equal,

00:05:01.962 --> 00:05:09.962
unequal and equal, and how can we test them? Well, we could ask, is 2 smaller than 3, and then the answer is

00:05:15.065 --> 00:05:17.065
yes, it's true. Then we have an ampersand symbol for AND,

00:05:16.488 --> 00:05:24.488
and a vertical bar, symbol for OR. And if we use ampersand or vertical bar once,

00:05:24.701 --> 00:05:32.701
it also applies to vectors, so basically it applies to every element of a vector, is an element-wise operator.

00:05:32.507 --> 00:05:38.507
Whereas if we use it twice, then it only applies to the first element of a vector,

00:05:45.363 --> 00:05:47.363
or just a scalar. And once again, here we could test if they work, we could ask R: "Is 2 smaller than 3 AND

00:05:47.566 --> 00:05:53.566
is 2 larger than 4?" and the answer is clearly false, because the second element

00:05:57.695 --> 00:05:59.695
is not satisfied here. Alright, how do you write R scripts?

00:06:04.023 --> 00:06:09.023
So definitely you could write them in the editor, so it was, the

00:06:15.259 --> 00:06:18.259
top left pane in RStudio. And then, these codes could be stored in files with ending .r.

00:06:17.742 --> 00:06:23.742
And then to run single lines from a script, we could just hit

00:06:24.484 --> 00:06:29.484
Ctrl and Enter, and then definitely I advise you to

00:06:35.008 --> 00:06:38.008
comment on your code, using this hashtag. So if I write hashtag, this line is a comment, then

00:06:40.644 --> 00:06:45.644
R knows that this is just a comment, and it shouldn't execute the operation in that particular line.

00:06:47.507 --> 00:06:53.507
Now, let us briefly talk about objects, so what is an object? An object in R

00:06:53.975 --> 00:06:58.975
can be nearly everything. It can be scalar, a matrix, a function, or even a plot.

00:06:59.244 --> 00:07:06.244
And the name of an object can be every combination of letters, numbers, and special characters.

00:07:09.808 --> 00:07:12.808
Names should begin with the letter, and Names are case sensitive.

00:07:17.143 --> 00:07:19.143
This is, like, something to be reminded while you code. This is very important.

00:07:18.695 --> 00:07:27.695
And, also overwriting objects causes no warning in R, something, another thing to have a note of.

00:07:33.056 --> 00:07:35.056
Now let us talk about the variable assignment. So, if we'd like to

00:07:35.116 --> 00:07:40.116
assign 2 to A, what do I do? I just write A

00:07:39.891 --> 00:07:43.891
and an arrow directed at A, and then I write 2.

00:07:44.006 --> 00:07:50.006
We could also use the equality symbol, so I could also use here B equals 2.

00:07:56.026 --> 00:08:01.026
This arrow could be directed in the different direction, so I could assign 2 to C.

00:08:00.743 --> 00:08:06.743
And now, always the arrow directs towards the name of a variable.

00:08:06.883 --> 00:08:13.883
And then we could also do the assignment in one line. So basically, I could write D is E is F is 3.

00:08:21.605 --> 00:08:25.605
And we could definitely also use the assign function. And while he printed the results, we could definitely

00:08:36.253 --> 00:08:39.253
print them, like, by asking to have printed C, E, and Y in one line, but this is not really recommended, it's not a great coding style to have them all in one line

00:08:46.042 --> 00:08:49.042
and with that semicolon. So, as you can see, there are many choices when it comes to variable assignment.

00:08:55.743 --> 00:08:57.743
This arrow is recommended in Headley Wickham's style guide,

00:09:02.549 --> 00:09:05.549
but of course select whatever works for you. When it comes to my personal usage,

00:09:11.487 --> 00:09:13.487
I use arrows for functions and this quality sign for variables,

00:09:19.766 --> 00:09:21.766
vectors and matrices. Now, if we would like to list

00:09:21.076 --> 00:09:33.076
all the objects, then we just can type ls and parentheses, and we have all the objects listed, so these are all the objects you saw.

00:09:32.894 --> 00:09:39.894
in the past slice: a, b, c, d, e, f, y.

00:09:46.285 --> 00:09:48.285
If we would like to remove one object from a workspace, then what do we type? We type

00:09:48.169 --> 00:09:53.169
rm(a), because I would like to remove object a, and indeed

00:09:59.050 --> 00:10:03.050
once again, once I type ls(), a is removed. If I would like to remove all objects, then we can write

00:10:08.945 --> 00:10:10.945
rm list equals to ls, and it removes all objects. And as you can see now, if I type ls,

00:10:19.886 --> 00:10:22.886
there are no objects in my workspace. Now let us briefly talk about data types. So, we could use, function mode applied to object,

00:10:29.050 --> 00:10:31.050
to get the object's type. So we have different types of objects. So, for example, we could have a character.

00:10:35.309 --> 00:10:39.309
A character is a string of arbitrary characters. And these are always enclosed in quotation marks

00:10:46.912 --> 00:10:48.912
like this one, or the other one. We could also have a numeric as a type, which is, for example, a real number.

00:10:49.024 --> 00:10:57.024
And, logical, true or false, and complex number, a function or a list. And here, as you can see, once I apply

00:11:00.805 --> 00:11:02.805
mode, for example, to that character string, to "Hallo", then I get

00:11:03.102 --> 00:11:09.102
character as an object type, if I applied it to TRUE, then I get logical

00:11:14.925 --> 00:11:16.925
as an object type. When it comes to the R main structures:

00:11:19.357 --> 00:11:23.357
We have vectors. Vectors are one-dimensional arrays of, for example, length m.

00:11:24.230 --> 00:11:28.230
And in vectors, all elements must be of the same type.

00:11:37.164 --> 00:11:41.164
Matrices are two-dimensional, m times n arrays. In the array, all elements must be of the same time once again.

00:11:45.683 --> 00:11:48.683
And then, an array: It's a generalization of matrices

00:11:54.504 --> 00:11:58.504
and it can be more than two-dimensional. Very useful data frames, so data frames are two-dimensional structures

00:11:59.051 --> 00:12:10.051
like matrices, but data frames allow different types in different columns, and this is very handy for data analysis. You'll see that you'll work a lot with

00:12:10.055 --> 00:12:20.055
data frames, and we have lists, once again, extremely popular, because this is a collection of objects of arbitrary types and structure, so very, very handy

00:12:24.978 --> 00:12:26.978
while doing data analysis. Now, let us talk about classes. So, the class of an object

00:12:26.856 --> 00:12:32.856
determines how it is treated, and this is related to object-oriented programming.

00:12:32.933 --> 00:12:37.933
And then to learn about the class of an object, we apply class

00:12:41.519 --> 00:12:45.519
to our object, and we get its particular class. So, all object types

00:12:51.023 --> 00:12:53.023
correspond to classes. A vector, it's not its own class. So, basically, if I apply

00:13:00.385 --> 00:13:02.385
class to a vector, it will return the type of its element. So, for example, if I have numbers, it will return

00:13:07.285 --> 00:13:09.285
"numeric", if I have some letters or names, it will return "character", etc.

00:13:10.903 --> 00:13:15.903
All other main structures listed here, apart from the vector,

00:13:22.269 --> 00:13:25.269
have their own distinct classes. And all the important classes include integer, so here we have integer values, and factor.

00:13:27.046 --> 00:13:32.046
Factor is categorical data stored as integers with associated labels.

00:13:37.102 --> 00:13:41.102
We have also these two, other handy functions, is.*() a certain name, and as.*() a certain name.

00:13:41.463 --> 00:13:46.463
So, basically, if we write is.*() and a certain class, we are asking:

00:13:47.037 --> 00:13:52.037
"Is a particular object of this certain class, so is this numeric?"

00:13:52.138 --> 00:13:57.138
If we apply it, for example, to 1, and then we have an answer, yes, this is numeric.

00:13:59.787 --> 00:14:01.787
If we apply is.character() to 1,

00:14:01.940 --> 00:14:05.940
then we have false, because it's clearly not character, it's numeric.

00:14:09.705 --> 00:14:11.705
as.*(), so as and the name of a class,

00:14:16.241 --> 00:14:19.241
this function attempts to coerce an object to

00:14:18.599 --> 00:14:23.599
a particular kind. So, for example, here, if I type as.character,

00:14:29.889 --> 00:14:31.889
it will try to coerce, so change, the class of this particular object to a

00:14:34.147 --> 00:14:38.147
character 1, and then indeed what has happened, it has done it. So now one is

00:14:42.789 --> 00:14:46.789
treated as a character, not a number anymore. Right, so,

00:14:53.475 --> 00:14:58.475
first of all here, what is a workspace? A workspace in R, is all the objects, vectors, scalars, functions, created in a particular session.

00:15:01.589 --> 00:15:04.589
And you might remember that in R studio, workspace was visible

00:15:09.259 --> 00:15:11.259
on the Environment tab.

00:15:10.957 --> 00:15:15.957
At the top right. And, when it comes to the history: History

00:15:22.981 --> 00:15:24.981
has all the commands typed in a session. So, once again, in RStudio, it was under history tab

00:15:27.407 --> 00:15:29.407
on the top right, once again.

00:15:31.045 --> 00:15:39.045
Now, a few other very handy functions. So, for example, if I would like to set a working directory, so basically where

00:15:38.499 --> 00:15:44.499
all the output should go to, then I could just type setwd(),

00:15:51.307 --> 00:15:53.307
and then my working directory. Then, if I would like to get a working directory, I type getwd().

00:15:54.156 --> 00:15:58.156
If I would like to save a workspace to an R data,

00:16:04.351 --> 00:16:06.351
then I type save.image(). And if you would like to, save a history, so all the commands,

00:16:10.457 --> 00:16:13.457
I type safehistory(). To load a workspace, I can just write load(),

00:16:18.153 --> 00:16:22.153
and then in the quotation marks, this R data. And if I would like to load the history, I can just type

00:16:21.801 --> 00:16:29.801
loadhistory(). And actually, once I do it, it enables me to browse through history

00:16:34.526 --> 00:16:37.526
in command line, using up and down. A few other handy functions, so source():

00:16:47.583 --> 00:16:51.583
What does source do? So, if I apply source to this R file, then basically source executes everything what's there. So functions, it just,

00:16:54.608 --> 00:16:58.608
saves variables to the environment, etc. Now, sink(). What does sink so? So, sink, first

00:16:58.697 --> 00:17:04.697
creates a file, and here the file will be called abc.txt,

00:17:10.088 --> 00:17:12.088
and then, it captures everything from the console output. So here, if I type

00:17:12.669 --> 00:17:17.669
sink(abc.txt), the file is created, then I type 3 plus 5.

00:17:20.569 --> 00:17:24.569
Then, in that particular file, I would have 8 captured. And then if I would like to close

00:17:33.782 --> 00:17:36.782
sink, then I just need to write sink(). And two other functions, very handy ones, q() and quit(), it

00:17:40.120 --> 00:17:43.120
allows us to quit R. Okay, and now let us

00:17:49.527 --> 00:17:51.527
talk about R packages. Packages are very useful, because they are bundles of functions and datasets,

00:17:52.281 --> 00:17:56.281
and before using a package, we need to install it on our machine.

00:17:56.297 --> 00:18:00.297
How do we do it? We can call this function install.packages,

00:18:03.051 --> 00:18:06.051
and then, in the quotation marks, we write the name of a package.

00:18:11.791 --> 00:18:13.791
To access a package's content, we must load it and attach it to our particular session.

00:18:14.453 --> 00:18:22.453
How do we do it? Well, we just type library(), and once again, the name of a package, or require(). And once again, the name of a package.

00:18:22.882 --> 00:18:28.882
Here, remember that every time we start a new session and we would like to access

00:18:29.401 --> 00:18:36.401
the package's content, we'd need to call it back, so using library or require, whereas installing packages

00:18:40.128 --> 00:18:42.128
is done only once. Library() throws an error,

00:18:46.101 --> 00:18:48.101
if the package is not found. Whereas, require() only gives us a warning

00:18:55.153 --> 00:18:58.153
and returns false. If you only want to use a single function from a package, you don't need to load it, actually,

00:18:59.783 --> 00:19:05.783
you can access that function directly. So how do we do it? We just type the name of a package

00:19:14.468 --> 00:19:17.468
:: and then the name of a function. Now, let us look at the example. So, I would like to install package mvtnorm,

00:19:20.465 --> 00:19:22.465
so install.packages("mvtnorm")

00:19:31.005 --> 00:19:32.005
Then, if I would like to, update packages, then I could also type update.packages.

00:19:34.475 --> 00:19:37.475
If I would like to remove packages from my machine. I could type remove.packages.

00:19:38.151 --> 00:19:44.151
Now, if I would like to show all installed libraries, I type library().

00:19:43.529 --> 00:19:51.529
And then, if I would like to load and attach library, and you are going to use it a lot while coding, then I write library("mvtnorm"),

00:19:57.502 --> 00:20:01.502
or require("mvtnorm"). Now, if I would like to use only one function from that particular package

00:20:07.194 --> 00:20:12.194
function here, dmvnorm, I call mvtnorm:: and then the name of a function, and then I call this particular

00:20:21.459 --> 00:20:25.459
function. We could also use search(), to show attached packages and data frames, and ls(2) and then here the second element, what does it do? Well, it shows us

00:20:28.059 --> 00:20:30.059
functions for the second package.

00:20:30.246 --> 00:20:33.246
How do we get help? Well, definitely, we can just type

00:20:32.552 --> 00:20:43.552
help(), and then this would display the help page for a topic. For example, if I would like to get some help for the sin function, then I just write

00:20:53.273 --> 00:20:55.273
help(sin). Question mark, and you might remember I used question mark in our video with the intro to RStudio,

00:21:00.837 --> 00:21:02.837
this is equivalent to help. If we type help.search(),

00:21:02.765 --> 00:21:07.765
then it searches the help system for a character string, so it would

00:21:09.290 --> 00:21:13.290
just basically show us all the functions, elements of the help system,

00:21:19.023 --> 00:21:22.023
where this string appears. And, ?? is equivalent to help search.

00:21:28.841 --> 00:21:33.841
help.start() opens the HTML version of R's documentation. And if we'd like to get some info about a particular package,

00:21:34.438 --> 00:21:38.438
then we could just type library(help = "name of the package").

00:21:38.805 --> 00:21:42.805
For example, if I would like to get some info about mvtnorm

00:21:44.814 --> 00:21:48.814
package, I just type library(help = "mvtnorm").

00:21:58.479 --> 00:22:01.479
Of course, to get even more help. We could just google it, or use any browser of our choice. We could look at Stack Overflow, or QuickR.

00:22:01.781 --> 00:22:11.781
Of course, nowadays, using chat boxes is extremely popular, and definitely people use it to even write entire chunks of code,

00:22:16.242 --> 00:22:22.242
it's very, very popular. And then we also have R mailing lists, but I would say these are less

00:22:28.923 --> 00:22:32.923
common among new users, I would say. Alright, and then, to sum it up, I will just conclude with a list of readings. Of course

00:22:39.219 --> 00:22:42.219
there are plenty of books and multitude of resources on R, because R is popular

00:22:48.978 --> 00:22:53.978
among many, many communities in research and industry. And there are many books, I would definitely

00:22:56.898 --> 00:23:00.898
recommend you to have a look at this Hadley Wickham's style guide.

00:23:00.371 --> 00:23:05.371
But please have a look, maybe you'll find something which is particularly

00:23:15.401 --> 00:23:17.401
amusing and interesting for you. Okay, so thank you for today, and I see you at the next video. Thank you.

